steps.body:
Some searches let `.` stand for any one letter, so `b.d` matches `bad` and `bed`. A plain walk can't follow a `.`, because it doesn't know which child to take. Search recursively instead, passing the node and the index of the next letter:

```python harness
class TrieNode:
    def __init__(self):
        self.children = {}
        self.is_end = False


root = TrieNode()
for word in ["bad", "bed"]:
    node = root
    for ch in word:
        node = node.children.setdefault(ch, TrieNode())
    node.is_end = True
{{code}}
print(matches(root, "b.d", 0), matches(root, "b.t", 0), matches(root, "..", 0))
```

```python
def matches(node, word, i):
    if i == len(word):
        return node.is_end
    ch = word[i]
    if ch == ".":
        return any(matches(child, word, i + 1) for child in node.children.values())
    child = node.children.get(ch)
    return child is not None and matches(child, word, i + 1)
```

When `i` reaches the end of the pattern, the match counts only if a word ends at this node. At a `.`, try every child and succeed if any branch does. `any()` stops at the first `True`, so it explores no more than it needs. At a normal letter there's only one child to follow.

A pattern of all dots can visit much of the trie, but every real letter narrows the search to one branch.

example.code:
class TrieNode:
    def __init__(self):
        self.children = {}
        self.is_end = False


def insert(root, word):
    node = root
    for ch in word:
        node = node.children.setdefault(ch, TrieNode())
    node.is_end = True


def matches(node, word, i):
    if i == len(word):
        return node.is_end
    ch = word[i]
    if ch == ".":
        return any(matches(child, word, i + 1) for child in node.children.values())
    child = node.children.get(ch)
    return child is not None and matches(child, word, i + 1)


root = TrieNode()
for word in ["pin", "pan", "pen", "spin", "pine"]:
    insert(root, word)
for pattern in ["p.n", ".pin", "pi..", "p..", "....", "s.n"]:
    print(pattern, matches(root, pattern, 0))

